WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Good morning John.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.949
Today’s Vlogbrothers is going to be a little
bit weird.

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:04.870
I am right now at an antique mall.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:10.910
I found a thing that I am so amazed by that
I just can’t help but take it apart.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.370
It is now in my home!

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.639
This thing is at least 25 pounds.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:21.210
It’s made entirely of metal, and it can
do addition and subtraction and kind of multiplication.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.210
Not really.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.590
Obviously, a calculator that can do addition
and subtraction these days is not impressive.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.040
But when it was purchased it probably cost
around as much as a really nice laptop today.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.470
I don’t know how this works, but that's
what I’m going to try and figure out today.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.489
There are a number of amazing things going
on all at once inside of this machine.

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:42.390
First, most simply, let's just put in the
number 99, that’s 99 cents.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.370
Both of the dials will count up to nine.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.699
If we add one to the 99 there, if we add one
cent, we’re gonna get a buck right?

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.340
Now, I know how the first column happens.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.239
There is a pin that tells in the machine that
to roll this dial, the first column dial,

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:00.239
one digit.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:01.829
Now that makes sense.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:08.550
But then it gets carried forward and that
is amazing to me.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.550
What?

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.760
How did this machine, that is a just a machine,
figure that out?

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.210
That's what we wanna know, and as far as I
am concerned, there's only one way to find

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.210
out.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.690
So let’s take it apart.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.210
[music]

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.740
Ugh.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.740
What is this?

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.840
Is it a dead animal?

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.440
Something crawled in there and died like a
thousand years ago?

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.130
Oh I have no idea.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.620
But I touched it.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.670
Now this thing comes off and…

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:39.670
I got one of these!

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:44.300
I should now be able to lift this off of the
mach-- I can!

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.340
It’s fuzzy on the inside.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.340
Oh man.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.040
Now this just got way cooler!

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.400
There’s been something rolling around in
here.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.400
I can hear it.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.400
It’s a dime!

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.400
1980, so it’s not that old.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.940
How does it stop at the right place?

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:57.940
How does it carry the number?

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.160
How does the subtract function work?

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.160
Nerdy?

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.160
Yes.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.160
Yes, it's nerdy.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.160
It is very nerdy.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.150
Each one of these parts had to be individually
stamped.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.740
How do you come off?

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.789
I hate flat head screws so much right now.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.819
A lot of these screws are really stuck, which
is not all that surprising.

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:16.569
You actually have to be depressing a button
in order to access this screw; so that’s

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.569
something.

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:18.799
Were those the only four screws?

00:02:18.799 --> 00:02:19.799
Yes!

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:22.000
Well something’s happened.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Whooooaaaaa.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Okay.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.049
This is the back of the number plate.

00:02:25.049 --> 00:02:33.109
When you push each of the numbers, it pops
down into one of those nine slots there.

00:02:33.109 --> 00:02:35.090
Ah!

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.090
I got it!

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.090
I figured it out!

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.829
When you push down a number, two things happen.

00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:44.439
One, that depression will push down this lever
a little bit, and that actually allows for

00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:49.299
this thing up here, the gear that actually
turns the number wheel, to actually start

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:50.299
moving.

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:54.530
How much it moves is a question of which key
got pushed down.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:59.330
Whichever button is pushed will actually block
that at a certain point if nothing stops it,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.709
it will go all nine, and you get nine.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:06.260
If it stops like half way in the middle then
it stops at five, and it will only count five.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.499
So this oil I have on my fingers right now
was put on here by someone in a factory in

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:11.499
the 1930s.

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:14.459
The numbers are somehow responsible for the
digit carrying.

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:16.939
Boy, am I glad I didn’t have to manufacture
these things for a living.

00:03:16.939 --> 00:03:18.800
I would have not been good at it.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:25.340
So at zero, this notch holds this pin, and
this pin, when you move it out of the way,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.249
it just automatically advances.

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:28.640
That pin is the secret.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.469
You know, it’s the kind of thing that we
would never make today but it has, in its

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:36.610
functionality, a beauty that I am a big fan
of.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:41.659
It does so many things all at once, and it
is powered only by the crank of the hand.

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:46.159
Thanks to all the people who, uh, you know,
we won't remember the names of who put all

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:48.279
their time and energy into the creation of
these devices.

00:03:48.279 --> 00:03:50.300
It’s just so very neat.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.309
So very, very neat.

00:03:52.309 --> 00:03:59.090
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

